
Firmware tests on the host
============================

Run from the repository root with PlatformIO Core and a host C++ compiler
(Apple Command Line Tools on macOS):

    pio test -e native

For the standard IDE installation, use ~/.platformio/penv/bin/pio instead of
pio if it is not on PATH. PlatformIO downloads its native platform and Unity
dependency on the first run. No CoreS3 is needed and this command never uploads.

The test_controller suite uses Unity. setUp() creates a fresh RobotStateMachine
value for each test; timestamps are explicit uint32_t millisecond values and
press inputs are individual events, not held-touch levels. No real sleeps or
hardware mocks are used by this suite. The native build_src_filter compiles
production RobotStateMachine.cpp, TranscriptionProtocol.cpp and RobotAudio.cpp;
Arduino main, RobotController, RobotTranscription, FaceRenderer and real
M5Unified are excluded. Audio uses the native-only test/support driver/heap
fixtures through an include path configured only in the native environment.
The firmware builds the same helper and supplies millis() and
M5.Touch.getDetail().wasPressed() at its hardware boundary.

The 19 controller cases cover readiness rejection without capturing, ten
capture/submit/result cycles with once-only action signals, empty results,
busy/held presses, and presses coincident with completion or late cleanup.
They exercise capture's 10999/11000 ms and transcription's 29999/30000 ms
boundaries, finalized capture precedence, once-only no-speech with no upload,
independent watchdog clocks, unsigned rollover, immediate admission
and worker failures, stale/late request IDs, and capture admission held until
transport acknowledgment. Playback facts cannot enter a speaking phase.
Idle-only sleep, repeated wake-to-idle, fresh-press recording, initialization,
and completion/failure inactivity refresh are covered. Pure readiness, pending,
ID and result facts carry no networking types across the native boundary.
See docs/plan/tap-to-transcribe.md for the accepted interaction and device checks.

The eight test_audio cases run production RobotAudio against the M5 two-slot
queue/callback and PSRAM allocation fixtures. They cover queued 20 ms frames,
speech beyond two seconds, 3-second initial silence and delayed onset, exact
120 ms cumulative activity, 4.98/5.00-second hangover/reset, quiet/brief noise,
exact 160,000-sample cap (including a quiet wait cut short by the cap),
sample-count playback/borrowing, buffer reuse and busy borrows, failed allocation/start/refill, retry, delayed final callback, and a
slow main update discarding captured tail only after driver join. The fixture
models completed PCM and driver ownership; it does not prove actual I2S/DMA
continuity or thread scheduling. Energy checks cover 99 PCM units below the
100-unit mean absolute threshold and 100 units at the threshold. This threshold
is an initial device-tuning value, not a validated speech detector.

The test_transcription suite exercises the same bounded HTTP parser used by
the worker, without a socket or RTOS mock. It covers numeric IPv4 base URLs,
fragmented multilingual UTF-8, empty/max-size results and embedded NUL lengths,
HTTP rejection/redirects, incomplete bodies, malformed or ambiguous framing,
media/encoding rejection, fixed header/line/body limits, canary-protected output,
and invalid/overlong/surrogate/out-of-range UTF-8. It does not establish socket
cleanup, task allocation, clip ownership under real concurrency, Wi-Fi memory
use or device timing. Those remain CoreS3 checks in the tap-to-transcribe plan.

Firmware compile check (also no upload):

    pio run -e m5stack-cores3

Native tests establish sample/state decisions, modeled driver handoff and
once-per-update action signals; actual capture/submission/Serial counts, length-based Serial output, concurrent
clip release, microphone quality, physical touch, renderer notifications, blink
recovery and networking require the silence-stop and transcription plans'
CoreS3 checks. The backend suite checks its process/HTTP boundary separately; T1 also records a
real local-model clip smoke. Neither command above flashes a device; a build
does not prove behavior.
